WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.048
How to run a Bloch-SIS-PoW node with snapshot sync at k=8.

2
00:00:04.048 --> 00:00:08.585
Bloch is an ownerless, post-quantum proof-of-work research chain.

3
00:00:08.585 --> 00:00:11.865
It is unaudited, and the coin carries no value.

4
00:00:12.315 --> 00:00:17.167
Why start from a snapshot? The Module-SIS gate is now active at k=8,

5
00:00:17.167 --> 00:00:20.877
which makes valid candidates about 4096 times rarer.

6
00:00:20.877 --> 00:00:25.443
That is a stronger structural filter — not a security guarantee.

7
00:00:25.443 --> 00:00:32.007
The old k=4 history is invalid under k=8 and pruned, so syncing from genesis is impractical.

8
00:00:32.007 --> 00:00:35.717
Instead, restore a signed snapshot and mine forward.

9
00:00:35.717 --> 00:00:38.214
Expect slow blocks at low hashrate.

10
00:00:38.214 --> 00:00:40.783
That is the gate working, not a bug.

11
00:00:41.248 --> 00:00:43.203
Step 1 — build from source.

12
00:00:43.203 --> 00:00:47.548
Clone the project from GitLab and run cargo build --release.

13
00:00:47.548 --> 00:00:51.603
You need stable Rust, plus clang, cmake, and pkg-config.

14
00:00:51.603 --> 00:00:53.920
The build produces two binaries:

15
00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:58.988
bloch — the node itself — and bloch-cli, for RPC calls and the wallet.

16
00:00:59.448 --> 00:01:01.551
Step 2 — create a miner address.

17
00:01:01.551 --> 00:01:05.230
Run bloch-cli newwallet with a path for the wallet file.

18
00:01:05.230 --> 00:01:08.646
It prompts for a password of at least 12 characters,

19
00:01:08.646 --> 00:01:11.734
and prints your address, starting with bloch1q…

20
00:01:11.734 --> 00:01:13.311
Back up the wallet file.

21
00:01:13.311 --> 00:01:16.004
Mining only ever uses the public address.

22
00:01:16.454 --> 00:01:22.307
Step 3 — download the snapshot into your data directory, and fetch the archive with curl.

23
00:01:22.307 --> 00:01:26.450
Always verify the SHA-256 checksum before trusting the archive.

24
00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:28.686
Once the check passes, extract it.

25
00:01:28.686 --> 00:01:33.289
The archive holds a db/ folder, which lands inside the data directory.

26
00:01:33.754 --> 00:01:35.255
Step 4 — run and mine.

27
00:01:35.255 --> 00:01:39.281
Point bloch at your data directory, add the bootstrap peer,

28
00:01:39.281 --> 00:01:42.010
and pass --mine with your miner address.

29
00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:47.127
The node loads the snapshot at around height 393,000 and mines k=8 forward.

30
00:01:47.127 --> 00:01:51.562
The bootstrap peer is only a convenience, not a privileged seed —

31
00:01:51.562 --> 00:01:53.677
the default seed list is empty.

32
00:01:54.127 --> 00:01:56.217
Step 5 — verify it is working.

33
00:01:56.217 --> 00:02:00.397
bloch-cli height should show roughly 393,000 — and climbing.

34
00:02:00.397 --> 00:02:06.248
bloch-cli peers should list at least one peer, and bloch-cli info gives an overview.

35
00:02:06.248 --> 00:02:11.333
To keep the node alive, wrap it in a systemd service with Restart=always.

36
00:02:11.794 --> 00:02:13.450
A final word of honesty:

37
00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:17.453
the k=8 gate is a structural filter, not a security claim.

38
00:02:17.453 --> 00:02:22.215
The chain is unaudited and 51%-attackable, and the coin has no value.

39
00:02:22.215 --> 00:02:26.493
The protocol is ownerless — Postern is one builder among many.

40
00:02:26.493 --> 00:02:30.151
If you need help, ask Vestnik on the Run-a-node page.

41
00:02:30.151 --> 00:02:31.531
Thanks for watching.

